

# Spring Transactions

## Notes

1. What is a transaction?
   Think of it like you send 1000 rs to your friend and your friend receives it.
   This is a transaction.
   Now again you send 1k but this time your friend has not received,
   and the money is also not credited to your account. Means it’s completely gone . to where no one knows.
   So, the transaction ensures either all operations are successful, or none of them happen.
   Without transaction db becomes inconsistent.

   Spring provides @Transactional annotation to automatically manage db transactions.

2. How Transactional works?
   @Transactional is a Spring annotation used to manage database transactions declaratively.
   When a method annotated with @Transactional is called, Spring creates an AOP proxy around the bean.
   The proxy starts a transaction before the method executes and either commits it if the method completes successfully or rolls it back if a runtime exception occurs.
   This ensures the ACID properties of transactions and keeps the database consistent. We typically place @Transactional on service-layer methods because a single business operation often involves multiple repository calls that should succeed or fail together.

